--TEST--
a deployment granted `net.connect` and `net.listen` may still not open a socket path, because `net.local` is a third grant neither of them widens — `rule:config/net-local-is-named-and-not-on-the-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The grant follows what the program is doing, not the transport it does it
// over — and a path is the one target `net.connect` cannot answer for, because
// that grant's whole character is the address policy it carries and a path has
// no address for the policy to read. Granting both network capabilities
// therefore buys a local socket nothing at all.
try {
    Core\Net::connectLocal("/run/novis/app.sock", 1s);
    echo "connected", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The same reading at the other end: `net.listen` is a grant over an endpoint,
// and a path is not one. A bind here asks the third grant like the dial above.
try {
    Core\Net::listenLocal("/run/novis/app.sock");
    echo "bound", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Both network grants, wide open, and no `local` key — which is
# `rule:security/capability-roster-is-closed`'s deny-by-default for the third.
[capabilities.net]
connect = true
listen = true
--EXPECT--
Core\Net::connectLocal needs the capability `net.local` for /run/novis/app.sock, which is not granted
Core\Net::listenLocal needs the capability `net.local` for /run/novis/app.sock, which is not granted
